{"id": "sevt_01MsC6uHNGzBRkS5aXRXMhoW", "content": [{"text": "**Cart p99 went from about 79 ms to 3,730 ms at 14:43, and the cause is the `df06e76` cart deploy.**\n\n**Deploys vs. metrics (last 6h)**\n- `checkout` `346f34d` deployed 14:41:00. Cart p99 was flat through it, so it's not the trigger.\n- `cart` `df06e76` (\"price cart lines individually\") deployed 14:43:20. Cart p99 jumps from 79 ms at 14:42 to 3,730 ms at 14:43. That is the gap: deploy 14:43:20, p99 moves 14:43.\n- `auth` `e8f9711` deployed 14:44:50, after the p99 jump, so it doesn't explain the spike.\n- `error_rate` stays at 0 until 14:52, then climbs to roughly 0.1\u20130.75%.\n\n**Diff**\n- The old code ran one batched query, `SkuPrice.objects.filter(sku_id__in=ids)`, then grouped the results in memory.\n- The new code runs `SkuPrice.objects.filter(sku_id=line.sku_id)` once per cart line. This is the \"per-row query where there used to be a batch\" pattern from the runbook.\n\n**Log confirmation**\n- Before the deploy, the log has only batched `sku_prices ... IN (?)` queries: 217 in total, averaging about 12 ms.\n- From 14:43 onward, the log has per-row `sku_prices ... sku_id = ?` queries: 1,888 in total, averaging about 13 ms each. Carts with several lines issue one query per line, up to 16 queries in a single request.\n- At 14:52 the cart DB pool hit its limit: `DB connection pool exhausted (active=50/50, waiters=12)`, followed by `500 ... PoolTimeout` on `/api/cart`. Those 500s are the source of the error rate. The pool is saturated because each request now holds connections across many sequential queries.\n\n**Root cause:** `df06e76` \u2014 replacing the single batched `sku_id__in` price lookup with one `SkuPrice` query per cart line multiplies DB round trips per request, which inflates cart p99 to ~4 s and exhausts the 50-connection pool, producing PoolTimeout 500s from 14:52.\n\nFix: revert `df06e76`, or restore the batched `sku_id__in` query and the in-memory grouping. Rolling back is the fastest path because the pool exhaustion is still driving errors. Confidence is high on the deploy-to-latency link. The per-request latency numbers are inferred from query counts and the pool errors; I did not measure end-to-end per-request timing.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T11:59:19.989250Z", "type": "agent.message"}
